{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bcc565b5b359e823", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# LP-CFM: Perceptual Invariance-Aware Conditional Flow Matching for Speech Modeling\narXiv: 2512.20314\nAuthors: Doyeop Kwak, Youngjoon Jang, Joon Son Chung\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: vocoder\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of Linear Projection Conditional Flow Matching (LP-CFM), a new formulation of conditional flow matching that models target distributions as projection-aligned elongated Gaussians along perceptually equivalent variants.\n  2. Derivation of line equations for speech modeling that incorporate perceptual invariances for amplitude scaling and temporal shifts.\n  3. Introduction of Vector Calibrated Sampling (VCS), a correction strategy that removes erroneous parallel components from predicted vectors to maintain consistency with the projection-based geometry.\n  4. Demonstration that LP-CFM consistently outperforms conventional optimal transport CFM in neural vocoding, with particularly strong improvements in low-resource and few-step sampling scenarios.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-NAR-Flow", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e3ff351e-1e9f-4de4-80ff-27ab3165dedc", "uuid": "bb926e63-2379-41f1-8339-bbbd6e276a71", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T19:09:45.730Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# LP-CFM: Perceptual Invariance-Aware Conditional Flow Matching for Speech Modeling\narXiv: 2512.20314\nAuthors: Doyeop Kwak, Youngjoon Jang, Joon Son Chung\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: vocoder\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of Linear Projection Conditional Flow Matching (LP-CFM), a new formulation of conditional flow matching that models target distributions as projection-aligned elongated Gaussians along perceptually equivalent variants.\n  2. Derivation of line equations for speech modeling that incorporate perceptual invariances for amplitude scaling and temporal shifts.\n  3. Introduction of Vector Calibrated Sampling (VCS), a correction strategy that removes erroneous parallel components from predicted vectors to maintain consistency with the projection-based geometry.\n  4. Demonstration that LP-CFM consistently outperforms conventional optimal transport CFM in neural vocoding, with particularly strong improvements in low-resource and few-step sampling scenarios.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-NAR-Flow", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
